-- Passage rg8-character-setting-plot-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6b2fe5e6-c276-51cd-a2b2-d275918cf54a', 'rg8-character-setting-plot-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Bridge That Held$q$, $q$Tomas had spent three weeks designing the balsa wood bridge, and he did not want anyone touching it. On Thursday afternoon, the engineering club gathered around the testing table in Room 114. Each team would hang weights from its bridge until something snapped.

His partner, Amara, picked up the bridge and turned it slowly in the light. "The middle joint is loose," she said. "If we test it like this, it will fail at the first weight."

"It's fine," Tomas said. He reached for the bridge, but she held it a moment longer.

"Ms. Delgado said we could have ten more minutes to fix problems," Amara said. "I would rather lose ten minutes than lose the whole bridge."

Tomas looked at the clock, then at the other teams. Two of them were already stacking weights. He thought about the hours he had spent gluing each piece by himself, and he felt his face grow warm. Amara had not even been at most of the work sessions.

Then he noticed what she was pointing at. A thin gap ran along the glue line, so faint that he had missed it every time he checked.

"Show me," he said quietly.

Together they added a small triangle of wood on each side of the joint and pressed it with clothespins while the glue set. When their turn came, the bridge held eight weights before the far end cracked. It was the second best score in the room.

On the bus home, Tomas opened his notebook and began a new design. This time, before he drew a single line, he wrote Amara's name at the top of the page beside his own.$q$, 'draft')
