-- Passage rg8-character-setting-plot-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('09820f7a-2d3a-5326-8651-577282d120d9', 'rg8-character-setting-plot-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Wrong Date$q$, $q$In the spring of 1894, Lucia Moreno worked mornings at her uncle's print shop on Water Street. Her job was to sort the metal letters back into their wooden cases after each job was printed. She was not allowed to set type herself. Her uncle said she was still too quick and not careful enough.

One Friday, the shop printed two hundred handbills for the town's library fair. The fair was set for Saturday, May 12. Lucia was folding the finished handbills when she noticed the line at the bottom. It read Saturday, May 21.

She looked across the room. Her uncle was bent over the press with a customer, a shipping clerk who needed receipts by noon. Lucia knew he hated to be interrupted. Last week he had snapped at her for asking where the string was kept.

She stood holding the stack for a long moment. Then she thought of the library committee hanging the handbills all over town, and of families arriving nine days late to an empty hall.

"Uncle Mateo," she said. "The date is wrong."

He did not look up. "The date is what the committee wrote."

"Then the committee wrote it wrong. The fair is on the twelfth. It says so on the notice in your own window."

This time he turned. He took one handbill, walked to the window, and read the notice twice. Then he pulled the frame of type from the rack without a word.

That afternoon, the shop reprinted every handbill. Before Lucia left, her uncle set a small wooden tool on her sorting table. It was a composing stick, the tool printers used for setting type.

"Monday," he said, "you start learning the careful way."$q$, 'draft')
