   $q$How does the point of view in Text 2 change the way the familiar pattern is presented?$q$, $q$[{"text":"A team member tells it using \"we,\" so the lesson feels like a shared experience.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Amara tells it herself, so the reader learns only her private thoughts and plans.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misidentifies-narrator"},{"text":"It is told the same way as Text 1, so the point of view does not change at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"An outside storyteller tells it, so the lesson is stated as a moral for all readers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-point-of-view"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A team member tells it using "we," so the lesson feels like a shared experience.$q$, $q$Text 2 uses "Our science fair team" and "we even tested it," so a teammate narrates, unlike the outside storyteller of Text 1. The narrator speaks of Amara as "she," so Amara is not the one telling it.$q$, 'draft', '470e60c2-9a22-544d-bada-45503f550c45')
