-- Passage rg8-connections-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8469820d-43cd-58be-a289-7f22795bd97b', 'rg8-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How a Glacier Moves$q$, $q$A glacier looks as still as a frozen lake, but it is always moving. Scientists often compare a glacier to a very slow river. Like a river, a glacier begins high in the mountains, flows downhill, and carries rocks and soil along with it. The biggest difference is speed. A fast river can travel several kilometers in an hour, while most glaciers move only a few centimeters to a few meters in a day.

A glacier forms where more snow falls each winter than melts each summer. Year after year, the new snow presses down on the older layers. The weight squeezes out the air and slowly turns the snow into dense blue ice. Once the ice becomes thick enough, gravity starts to pull it down the slope.

Here the river comparison begins to break down. Water flows because it is a liquid. Ice is a solid, yet deep inside a thick glacier the pressure is so great that the ice bends and creeps like cold putty. Near the bottom, a thin film of meltwater can also let the whole mass slide over the rock beneath it.

Rivers and glaciers also shape the land in different ways. A river cuts a narrow channel, which is why river valleys are often shaped like the letter V. A glacier is wide and heavy, so it scrapes the entire valley floor and both walls. When the ice finally melts away, it leaves behind a broad valley shaped like the letter U.

In the end, both a river and a glacier are moving parts of the water cycle. One simply works on a schedule measured in minutes, and the other works on a schedule measured in centuries.$q$, 'draft')
