  ('d913390d-6bce-511d-a7c5-19d6b43531ee', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Why do historians describe the canal and the railroad as "two chapters of the same story"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The same company built the canal first and then the railroad along its old route.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The two competed for exactly one year before the canal finally had to close.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The railroad tracks were laid on the towpath after the last canal boat stopped.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Each one in turn solved the problem of distance and changed where people lived and worked.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Each one in turn solved the problem of distance and changed where people lived and worked.$q$, $q$The last paragraph says each "solved the problem of distance" and "reshaped where the people of Harlow Falls lived and worked," one after the other like chapters. The towpath became a walking trail, not a rail line.$q$, 'draft', 'cf8e6bc1-de38-5922-9a3a-00d624025b0d')
