-- Passage rg8-context-clues-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('337b6a72-8c9c-5cc9-bd7e-7d39ff42813c', 'rg8-context-clues-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Mural on Pine Avenue$q$, $q$The wall behind the Pine Avenue laundromat had been gray for as long as Leilani could remember. So when Ms. Varga announced that the eighth-grade art class would paint a mural there, Leilani was thrilled. Her friend Dmitri was more skeptical. "The owner will never agree," he said, folding his arms. "And even if he does, it will rain the whole time."

To everyone's surprise, the owner agreed right away. The next challenge was the design. Twenty-six students each wanted to paint something different: oceans, planets, soccer players, a giant cat. Ms. Varga said the mural needed to be cohesive. "It can have many parts," she explained, "but they have to fit together and feel like one picture, not twenty-six separate ones."

The class chose a single subject: the neighborhood itself. Each student would paint one small scene from Pine Avenue.

Yusuf, who was new to the school, made a tentative suggestion during the planning meeting. He spoke quietly and kept glancing at the floor, as if he expected someone to reject the idea. "What if a river runs behind every scene," he said, "so it ties them together?" Ms. Varga wrote it on the board immediately.

The painting took three weekends. When it was done, the wall was vibrant with color: bright oranges, deep greens, and a winding river of brilliant blue. Even Dmitri had to admit it was better than he had imagined. People stopped on the sidewalk to take pictures, and the laundromat owner brought out lemonade for the painters.$q$, 'draft')
