   $q$When Tomas looks at the soggy tablecloth and says "Perfect" and "Just the weather I ordered," what does he actually mean?$q$, $q$[{"text":"He is pleased because he was hoping for a rainy day.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"He had asked a weather service to send rain that day.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading-extreme"},{"text":"He thinks the wet tablecloth still looks very nice.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-target"},{"text":"He is annoyed that the rain is ruining their plans.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$He is annoyed that the rain is ruining their plans.$q$, $q$This is verbal irony: Tomas says the opposite of what he feels while staring at a puddle under the cash box. Reading his words as sincere pleasure ignores the soggy scene that shows he is frustrated.$q$, 'draft', '80735e54-7cea-5d0d-b576-3115f19803a0')
