  ('efd52908-f809-5d53-8cf1-cd18575095f3', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The class traced the road "the tiny traveler had flown." Why does the author call the butterfly a "traveler" instead of an "insect"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Traveler\" shows that the butterfly made the full round trip.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"\"Traveler\" makes the butterfly sound like a pest to avoid.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-connotation"},{"text":"\"Traveler\" makes the butterfly sound like an adventurer on a journey.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Traveler\" is the scientific name for a monarch butterfly.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Traveler" makes the butterfly sound like an adventurer on a journey.$q$, $q$Both words can name the butterfly, but "traveler" gives it a human, adventurous feeling that builds admiration. The passage says "No single butterfly makes the full round trip," so the word does not mean that.$q$, 'draft', '89b4504f-6945-5d63-a6e0-b236c3d2ed1f')
