   $q$The poem begins and ends with the same two lines. How does their meaning change at the end?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They mean exactly the same thing both times, because the street never really changed.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misses-shift"},{"text":"At first they are spoken by visitors; at the end they are spoken by Mrs. Ito alone.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-speaker-character"},{"text":"At first they sound like a fact; at the end they open a story that proves them wrong.","is_correct":true},{"text":"At first they sound hopeful; at the end they show that the garden has finally died.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$At first they sound like a fact; at the end they open a story that proves them wrong.$q$, $q$At the start, the lines describe how the neighbors saw the lot; at the end, the children use them as the start of a story about a garden that did grow. The garden is green and full in the final stanza, so it has not died.$q$, 'draft', '8f1dbff1-9f04-534f-a72c-c1ab1455c97d')
