-- Passage rg8-main-idea-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6de16dd4-85f3-5c0d-b068-0e228c072659', 'rg8-main-idea-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Practice That Happens in Bed$q$, $q$Athletes spend hours running drills, lifting weights and studying game film. Yet one of the most important parts of training happens when they are not moving at all. Research on sleep suggests that rest is not time away from practice. It is part of how the body and brain improve.

During deep sleep, the body releases substances that help repair muscles. Hard exercise creates tiny tears in muscle fibers, and the repair work that follows is what makes muscles stronger. When an athlete skips sleep, that repair has less time to happen, and sore muscles may stay sore longer.

Sleep also helps the brain store new skills. When a basketball player practices a new shot, the brain builds connections that guide the movement. Studies have found that people who sleep after learning a physical task often perform it more smoothly the next day than people who stay awake. In other words, some of the learning from practice is finished at night.

A tired athlete also makes more mistakes. Reaction times slow down, and it becomes harder to focus on a play or remember a coach's instructions. Some studies of young athletes have found that those who regularly slept fewer than eight hours were more likely to get hurt.

For these reasons, many coaches now treat sleep as part of the training plan. They encourage players to keep a regular bedtime, put screens away before sleeping and avoid late practices when possible. One high school track coach, Ms. Oyelaran, even asks her runners to log their sleep alongside their running times. A good night's rest, she tells them, is a workout they cannot afford to skip.$q$, 'draft')
