-- Passage rg8-main-idea-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('90c26621-3e85-58db-9bd3-5e656a013904', 'rg8-main-idea-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Bringing a Mural Back$q$, $q$For forty years, a painted mural of a river and a crowd of fishing boats covered the side of the community center in Port Alder. Over time, sun and rain faded its blues into gray, and patches of paint peeled away. When the town decided to restore the mural, it hired a conservator named Ana Kowalczyk. Her job was not to paint a new picture. It was to bring back the original one as faithfully as possible.

Restoration begins with careful study. Kowalczyk photographed every section of the wall and compared the images with old photos from the town archive. She tested tiny paint samples to learn what materials the first artist had used. These steps helped her see which areas had faded, which had been painted over and which were still in good shape.

Next came cleaning and repair. Using soft brushes and mild cleaners, her team removed dirt without scrubbing away the original paint. They filled cracks in the wall and glued loose flakes of paint back into place before adding anything new.

Only then did the team begin repainting. They matched colors to the old photographs and added paint only where the original was missing. Kowalczyk kept a detailed record of every change, so future conservators would know which brushstrokes were new.

Finally, the team covered the mural with a clear coating that blocks some sunlight and makes graffiti easier to remove. The restoration took most of a summer. When it was finished, longtime residents said the boats looked the way they remembered from childhood. The careful, step-by-step process had given the town back a piece of its history rather than a copy of it.$q$, 'draft')
