-- Passage rg8-main-idea-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9ad029f5-7bf7-56c1-af7e-16352d0a74d7', 'rg8-main-idea-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Cooking Belongs in Every School$q$, $q$Many students can solve equations and write essays, yet they cannot cook a simple meal. Schools should change that by offering basic cooking classes to every student before high school graduation. Cooking is a practical skill that supports health, independence and learning in other subjects.

First, cooking helps young people make healthier choices. A student who knows how to prepare a bowl of soup or a stir-fry is less likely to depend on fast food or snacks from a vending machine. Learning to read recipes and nutrition labels also teaches students what is actually in their food.

Second, cooking builds independence. Within a few years, many students will live on their own for the first time. Knowing how to plan meals, follow a budget at the grocery store and store leftovers safely can save money and stress. These are skills that adults use every week.

Cooking also connects to other classes. Doubling a recipe requires fractions. Baking bread shows how yeast produces gas that makes dough rise. Exploring dishes from different cultures can lead to lessons about geography and history. A kitchen can be a lab, a math room and a social studies class all at once.

Some people argue that schools do not have the time or money for cooking classes. That concern is reasonable, since kitchens and ingredients cost money. However, a class does not require a fancy kitchen. Portable burners, shared ingredients and partnerships with local farms can keep costs low. Even a short unit within an existing health class would be a strong start.

Students leave school prepared to take tests. They should also leave prepared to feed themselves.$q$, 'draft')
