-- Passage rg8-story-details-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('391e7083-ed94-521f-a6b6-1693679227c9', 'rg8-story-details-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Crow and the Buried Acorns$q$, $q$In a valley of oaks lived a crow named Tamsin who loved to count. Every autumn she gathered acorns and lined them up along a fallen log, and every evening she counted them twice. Two hundred and twelve, she would say, and then she would sleep well.

Below her log lived a gray squirrel named Odo. He gathered acorns too, but he never counted them. He simply dug small holes all over the hillside, dropped an acorn into each one, and patted the soil flat with his paws.

"You will never find half of those," Tamsin told him one morning. "What a waste of good food."

Odo shrugged. "I find enough," he said.

The winter was long. Tamsin ate her acorns carefully, one row at a time, until the log was bare. Odo dug up what he could remember and left the rest asleep under the snow.

When spring arrived, Tamsin flew over the hillside and stopped in surprise. Tiny green shoots stood all across the slope, each one with two bright leaves. She landed beside the nearest one and tilted her head. A split acorn shell still clung to its stem.

That autumn, the crow did something new. She gathered her acorns, counted them twice, and then carried ten of them to the hillside. She pushed each one into the soil with her beak.$q$, 'draft')
