-- Passage rg8-story-details-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1c740122-60e8-56b7-939d-be257da8f1d4', 'rg8-story-details-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Mapmaker's Apprentice$q$, $q$In the spring of 1868, Ada Lindqvist swept the floor of Mr. Pell's map shop every morning before the sun cleared the harbor. She was fourteen, and she had been hired to sweep, to run errands, and to keep her hands off the copper plates. The plates were engraved with coastlines so fine that a single scratch could put a reef in the wrong place.

Ada kept her hands off the plates. She did not keep her eyes off them.

Each evening, after Mr. Pell went upstairs, she studied the newest chart of the bay. She had grown up on the fishing docks, and she knew the bay the way she knew her own kitchen. On the chart, the sandbar near Gull Point was drawn as a narrow line close to shore. Ada had watched her father steer wide around that sandbar for years. In her memory, it reached much farther out, almost to the channel marker.

She thought about saying something for three days. On the fourth day, she drew the point on a scrap of brown paper, marking the sandbar the way her father described it. She left the paper on Mr. Pell's desk beside his spectacles.

The next morning, the scrap was gone. Mr. Pell said nothing about it. He handed her the broom as usual. But that afternoon he rented a rowboat, and he did not come back until the tide was low.

A week later, Ada arrived to find a second stool beside the engraving table.$q$, 'draft')
